1. Number of patterns in which three pieces are placed consecutively (only continue placement along the original board, no lifting or overlapping): There are A1 ways to do this; 

2. Number of patterns in which the piece is placed symmetrically about the vertical or horizontal center line of the table: There are A2 ways to do this; 

3. Number of patterns in which three pieces are placed consecutively, and the pattern is distinct from both A1 and A2: There are A3 ways to do this. Then $A4 = A1 + A2 + A3 + 2$, because any of the four patterns in A1, A2 can be flipped, which results in two more different patterns. So $A1 = A2 + 2$, $A3 = A1 - A4 + A2 = 2(A2 - 1)$, $A4 = 3A2 - 2$, $3A2 - 2 = A3 + A4$, so $A2 = 2$, $A1 = A2 + 2 = 4$, $A3 = A1 - A4 + A2 = A2 - 1 = 1$, $A4 = 3A2 - 2 = 4$. Therefore, A1 = 4, A2 = 2, A3 = 1, A4 = 4, A5 = A3 + A4 = 5. So there are 4 + 2 + 1 + 4 + 5 = 16 patterns in total.</think> <answer> 16</answer>

